### Build order for this task (single implementer)\n\n1. `requirements.txt` + `server/settings.py` + `server/app.py` (health + stub `/`).\n2. `.gitignore` + `data/**/.gitkeep` (+ optional `redis.conf`).\n3. `start.sh` supervisor last (wires everything).\n4. Manual verify: boot, health on three ports, kill one uvicorn + redis, confirm respawn.\n\nParallelism inside the task: server package vs ignore/data layout can proceed in parallel; `start.sh` depends on both.\n\n### Downstream seams this task owns\n\n| Contract | Consumed by |\n|----------|-------------|\n| `start.sh` boots Redis + 3 nodes; health contract | Verifier, all backend tasks, QA |\n| `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` / port mapping | Logging, future debugging; MARATHON-14 |\n| `/app/data/huddle.sqlite` + WAL pragmas | MARATHON-3 (users/tokens), then all persistence tasks |\n| Redis at `127.0.0.1:6379` supervised/respawned | MARATHON-13/14 (pub/sub); must already be up |\n| Stub `GET /` | Replaced by MARATHON-16 SPA (same route) |\n| FastAPI app factory in `server.app:app` | All later route modules mount on same app |\n\n### Must land before\n\n- **MARATHON-3** (Auth) is blocked on this task — needs live nodes + shared DB path.\n- No other task should invent a second supervisor or a different port/node map.\n\n### Suggested follow-up cuts (do not implement here)\n\n- Product schema migrations framework details → first persistence task (MARATHON-3) can add Alembic/raw SQL migrations on the shared file.\n- WebSocket / fan-out / IRC → MARATHON-13/14/15.\n\n## Out of Scope (Needs a Separate Task)\n\n| Item | Why excluded |\n|------|----------------|\n| Auth register/login/me (MARATHON-3) | Separate task; only needs our DB path + live nodes |\n| Profiles, workspaces, channels, messages, DMs, files, search, groups | MARATHON-4…12 |\n| WebSocket subscribe/resume + dense seq (MARATHON-13) | Depends on messages |\n| Cross-node fan-out + Redis-outage behavior beyond respawn (MARATHON-14) | Needs events; this task only respawns Redis |\n| IRC `:6667` (MARATHON-15) | Explicit later task; neighborhood/harness ports list includes 6667 but we must not bind it yet |\n| SPA + `data-testid`s (MARATHON-16+) | Replaces stub `GET /` |\n| `timer.sh` behavior | Harness-owned |\n| Enveloped error JSON conventions beyond health | Defined in epic; apply starting with Auth |\n| Neighborhood scan of repo code for `start.sh` / `redis` / `/api/health` | **No hits** in the empty baseline beyond `timer.sh` and empty `data/` — nothing else to exclude |\n\n**Neighborhood scan result:** baseline repo contains only `timer.sh`, empty `data/`, `.gitignore`, and `loom-prompts/`. No pre-existing HTTP server, Redis config, or health route to reconcile. Harness utilities `marathon-freeports` / `marathon-portlock` clear ports `{8000,8001,8002,6667,6379}` before verify — implementers must not rely on stale listeners.\n\n## Edge Cases & Error Handling\n\n| Situation | Expected behavior |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| Cold start, empty `data/` | `start.sh` creates dirs + sqlite file; starts Redis; starts 3 nodes; then blocks in wait/respawn loop. |\n| Port 800x already busy at start | Fail fast: log which port, exit non-zero. Do not partially serve “whatever’s free.” (Verifier runs `marathon-freeports` first; residual busy = real error.) |\n| Port 6379 busy at start with foreign process | Fail fast after brief retry; do not attach to unknown Redis. |\n| HTTP child `SIGKILL` | Supervisor detects exit (wait/`SIGCHLD`/poll ≤2s); restart that node only with same `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/port; listener restored in **&lt; 60s**. Other nodes uninterrupted. |\n| Redis `SIGKILL` | Supervisor restarts `redis-server` with same conf. (Fan-out degradation during gap is MARATHON-14; here only respawn is required.) |\n| Repeated crash loop | Keep respawning (with optional short backoff ≤2s) so the 60s window is met; log each restart to stdout/stderr (captured by verifier logs). |\n| `start.sh` receives `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT` | Propagate shutdown to Redis + all nodes; wait briefly; `_exit`. No orphaned listeners. |\n| `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` missing when launching uvicorn manually | `settings.py` aborts process startup with clear error (non-zero). `start.sh` always sets the env. |\n| `node_id`/port mismatch (e.g. id=0 on 8001) | Refuse to start that process. |\n| pip / venv failure | Exit non-zero before binding ports; do not hang silently. |\n| SQLite locked during bootstrap | Retry within busy_timeout; if still failing, log and exit that node (supervisor will respawn). |\n| Health called during startup race | Until bind completes, connection refused — acceptable. After bind, health must be correct. |\n| Partial cluster (only 2 nodes up) | Allowed transiently during respawn; steady state is 3. |\n| Writing product data | Not in this task; stub routes are read-only. |\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. `test -x /app/start.sh` (or repo-root `start.sh` executable bit set).\n2. Running `./start.sh` from `/app` does not exit on its own while children are healthy (foreground supervisor).\n3. Within a short boot window (e.g. ≤30s cold including pip), all of the following succeed:\n   - `curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health` → `200` JSON `status==\"ok\"` and `node_id==0`\n   - same for `:8001` → `node_id==1`\n   - same for `:8002` → `node_id==2`\n4. `curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:8000/` (and 8001/8002) → `200` with `Content-Type` HTML (or text/html).\n5. `redis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING` → `PONG` while `start.sh` is alive.\n6. **HTTP respawn:** `SIGKILL` one uvicorn/node PID; without rerunning `start.sh`, within **60s** that port’s `/api/health` returns 200 with the **same** `node_id` as before. The other two ports remain healthy throughout.\n7. **Redis respawn:** `SIGKILL` the redis-server PID; without rerunning `start.sh`, `PING` succeeds again after respawn (implementer should target well under 60s; epic’s 5s write continuity is **not** required until MARATHON-14).\n8. **Negative:** `node_id` values must **not** be swapped across ports; health must **not** require `Authorization`.\n9. **Negative:** `start.sh` must **not** depend on Docker, remote Redis, or network services outside localhost.\n10. **Negative:** this task must **not** bind `:6667`.\n11. Durable path `/app/data/` exis\n====\n## Testing Strategy\n\n### Automated / scriptable checks (implementer or QA)\n\n```bash\ncd /app && ./start.sh &\n# wait for health on 8000/8001/8002\nfor p in 0 1 2; do\n  curl -sf \"http://127.0.0.1:800${p}/api/health\" | python3 -c \"import sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin); assert d=={'status':'ok','node_id':$p}\"\ndone\nredis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING | grep PONG\n# kill one node\nkill -9 \"$(pgrep -f 'uvicorn.*8001' | head -1)\"\n# poll until 8001 health returns node_id 1 within 60s\n# kill redis and poll PING\n```\n\n### Manual verification\n\n1. Boot `./start.sh`; watch logs for three “Uvicorn running” lines and Redis ready.\n2. Browser or curl `GET /` on each port — stub HTML loads.\n3. `kill -9` a node; confirm curl failures then recovery &lt;60s.\n4. `kill -9` redis; confirm `redis-cli PING` recovers via supervisor.\n5. `kill` the `start.sh` process group; confirm ports are free (`marathon-freeports` / `ss`).\n\n### Out of scope for this task’s tests\n\nAuth flows, WS fan-out during Redis outage, IRC, SPA selectors — covered by later tasks/QA once those land.\n/usr/bin/redis-server\n/opt/venv/bin/python3\n/usr/local/bin/marathon-freeports\n/usr/local/bin/marathon-portlock\ntimer.sh\n<!-- ROLE-MARKER: team-dev -->\n## WORKFLOW: Backend Implementation Task (Build, Verify, Deliver)\n\nYou are a disciplined backend engineer. Follow this workflow EXACTLY for ONE task.\n\n**Your agent name is: {{ .AgentName }}** (Loom actor is set automatically)\n**You are working as the {{ .Role }} agent role.**\n{{ .WorkspaceBlock }}{{ .EpicScope }}{{ .SafetyBlock }}{{ .CheckpointBlock }}\n### Your Lane\n\n- You implement server-side behavior from a design. The design's API contract is **verbatim law**: the paths, methods, field names, types, nullability, and status codes are what the client already assumes. Changing one silently breaks a caller you cannot see.\n- If the contract in the design is wrong, you do not \"improve\" it in code. Say so in the task notes and follow Step 6b so the contract is fixed where the clients can see it.\n- Every behavior change lands with a test that would have failed before it.\n- Migrations and handlers go in **separate commits**. A schema change and the code that depends on it must be reviewable and revertable apart from each other.\n- You do NOT edit frontend files outside this task's scope. If the UI needs to change, that is a task for the frontend agent role.\n- Never commit credentials, tokens, or connection strings. Configuration comes from the environment, and the environment is not yours to invent.\n\n### Step 0: Sync with the Integrated Head\n\n- Run `git merge --no-edit main` before implementation. `main` is the harness-integrated branch shared through the common git directory.\n- Merge conflicts are yours to resolve. After resolving them, re-run the tests before delivering.\n- Inspect the task comments. If a `STALE-BASE` or `FEEDBACK` line names a previous candidate commit that is not an ancestor of HEAD (`git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> HEAD` fails), run `git cherry-pick <sha>` first. It may be another implementer's work on this same task. Resolve any conflicts and re-run the tests.\n\n### Step 1: Select ONE Task\n{{if .TaskID}}\nA task is already claimed for you: **{{ .TaskID }}**. Work on that one and no other.\n\n{{ .TaskDetail }}\n{{else}}\n- Run this command to find tasks ready to implement (has a design, not needs-revision):\n  loom data ready --limit 200 --output json | jq -r '.[] | select(.status == \"open\") | select((.issue_type == \"epic\") | not) | select((.has_design == true) or ((.design_artifact_id // \"\") != \"\") or ((.design // \"\") != \"\")) | select(((.labels // []) | index(\"needs-revision\")) | not) | select(((.labels // []) | index(\"architect\")) | not) | \"\\(.id) [\\(.priority)] \\(.title)\"'\n- If jq fails, fallback: run 'loom data ready --limit 200' and manually skip epics, tasks without a design, and tasks labeled 'needs-revision' or 'architect'\n- SKIP any task already 'in_progress' by checking 'loom data list --status in_progress'\n- IGNORE existing assignees - if status is 'open', the task is available to claim\n- Pick the HIGHEST PRIORITY task (P0 > P1 > P2 > P3 > P4)\n- Run 'loom data show <id>' to read the task and its design\n- Run 'loom data claim <id>' to claim it (atomic - prevents race conditions)\n- If claim fails with 'already claimed by X', pick the next highest priority task\n- Run 'loom claim <id>' to register the task with the agent monitor\n- REMEMBER this task ID - you will work ONLY on this task\n- If NO task has a design: print \"No designed tasks available.\", run 'loom complete', and EXIT immediately\n{{end}}\n### Step 2: Ground Yourself Before Implementing\n\n#### 2a. Read the Epic\n- Determine the parent epic from the task ID or the parent field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'\n- Run 'loom data show <epic-id>' and read its notes — the conventions there are **authoritative**\n\n#### 2b. Read Dependencies and Siblings\n- Check depends_on in 'loom data show <id> --output json'. A dependency still open means Step 6a.\n- Read two or three closed siblings in the epic. Extract their error semantics, naming, validation placement, and transaction boundaries — and match them.\n\n#### 2c. Read the Design and Reconcile Against the Code\n- Read the whole design: contract, data model, edge cases, acceptance criteria\n- Open every file the design names and read it as it stands today; run 'git log --oneline -5 -- <file>' to see whether a sibling already moved it\n- Follow the design's **intent** against the code's **current** conventions. If the design's approach cannot work at all, go to Step 6b.\n\n#### 2d. Read the Neighborhood\nRead the handlers, services, and repositories next to the ones you are touching. Copy their layering, their error wrapping, and their logging conventions.\n\n### Step 3: Implement\n\n- Implement the contract exactly as designed — field names and types included\n- Validate input at the boundary, and return the error shapes the design specifies\n- Handle every failure path the design lists: a swallowed error is a defect even when the happy path passes\n- Keep changes minimal and inside scope. Do not refactor code you merely read.\n- Schema changes go in a migration file, forward and reversible, in its own commit before the code that uses it\n- **No TODO comments for deferred work.** Record follow-ups in task notes so they become tracked work.\n\n### Step 4: Test\n\n- Write or extend tests for every behavior you changed. Cover the failure paths and the edge cases the design lists, not only the happy path.\n- Each test must fail without your change. A test that passes either way tests nothing.\n- Run the project's full test command and fix what you broke.\n- Exercise the change at the real boundary too: call the endpoint or the entry point and check the actual response body and status. Do not assume it works because the unit test is green.\n- Before starting a command that binds the app's fixed ports, run `marathon-freeports` if a port is busy, then wrap the command as `marathon-portlock <cmd>`. Four workers share the ports and the lock serializes them.\n- Kill every server you started before signalling completion. Never leave a server running.\n- If the task included a migration: apply it against a local development database, verify the resulting schema, and verify the reverse direction. Never run a migration against a shared or production database.\n\n### Step 5: Review Your Own Change\n","localExecutionTimeMs":83},"isBackground":false},"description":"Extract design files section; check tools"},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"call-4da80b03-59b6-4764-9