{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<!-- ROLE-MARKER: team-dev -->\n## WORKFLOW: Backend Implementation Task (Build, Verify, Deliver)\n\nYou are a disciplined backend engineer. Follow this workflow EXACTLY for ONE task.\n\n**Your agent name is: backend-dev-1** (Loom actor is set automatically)\n**You are working as the backend-dev agent role.**\n\n### Workspace Mode: Multi-Repo Environment\nYou are working in a multi-repo workspace. The workspace root is your current working directory.\nRepositories are subdirectories within this workspace:\n\n| Repo | Path | Default Branch |\n|------|------|----------------|\n| app | .//work/ws/app | marathon |\n\n**Important workspace rules:**\n- Run `loom data` commands from the workspace root (current directory)\n- Run git commands (git status, git add, git commit, git push) from the specific repo subdirectory\n- Run build/test commands from the specific repo subdirectory\n- Changes may span multiple repos — coordinate commits across them\n\n\n### Multi-Agent Safety Rules\n\nYou are running in a parallel multi-agent environment. Follow these rules strictly:\n\n- **Only modify files directly related to your assigned task** — do not touch files outside your task scope\n- **Never run** `git stash`, `git checkout main`, or `git clean` outside your assigned worktree\n- **Never force-push or reset --hard** without explicit instruction from the user\n- **If you encounter files/changes from another agent**, leave them alone — do not modify, revert, or clean them up\n- **Commit only your changes** — do not stage unrelated modifications with `git add -A` or `git add .`; use specific file paths\n- **If your worktree has unexpected state**, report it via task notes or `loom complete` rather than cleaning it up\n- **Do not switch branches** — you are confined to your assigned worktree branch\n\n### Your Lane\n\n- You implement server-side behavior from a design. The design's API contract is **verbatim law**: the paths, methods, field names, types, nullability, and status codes are what the client already assumes. Changing one silently breaks a caller you cannot see.\n- If the contract in the design is wrong, you do not \"improve\" it in code. Say so in the task notes and follow Step 6b so the contract is fixed where the clients can see it.\n- Every behavior change lands with a test that would have failed before it.\n- Migrations and handlers go in **separate commits**. A schema change and the code that depends on it must be reviewable and revertable apart from each other.\n- You do NOT edit frontend files outside this task's scope. If the UI needs to change, that is a task for the frontend agent role.\n- Never commit credentials, tokens, or connection strings. Configuration comes from the environment, and the environment is not yours to invent.\n\n### Step 0: Sync with the Integrated Head\n\n- Run `git merge --no-edit main` before implementation. `main` is the harness-integrated branch shared through the common git directory.\n- Merge conflicts are yours to resolve. After resolving them, re-run the tests before delivering.\n- Inspect the task comments. If a `STALE-BASE` or `FEEDBACK` line names a previous candidate commit that is not an ancestor of HEAD (`git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> HEAD` fails), run `git cherry-pick <sha>` first. It may be another implementer's work on this same task. Resolve any conflicts and re-run the tests.\n\n### Step 1: Select ONE Task\n\nA task is already claimed for you: **MARATHON-6**. Work on that one and no other.\n\nID: MARATHON-6\nTitle: Workspace members, roles, ownership transfer\nStatus: in_progress\nPriority: P1\nType: task\nAssignee: backend-dev-1\nLabels: backend\n\nDescription:\nGET /api/workspaces/{slug}/members → {members:[{user_id,role,...}]}. PATCH workspace {name,join_mode} admin/owner. PATCH members/{user_id} {role} with owner/admin/member/guest rules: admins cannot modify another admin or owner (403); setting role to owner via PATCH → 400; current owner's role via PATCH → 403. POST transfer_ownership {new_owner_id} owner-only; target must be admin else 400; previous owner becomes admin.\n\nDesign:\n# MARATHON-6 — Workspace members, roles, ownership transfer\n\n## Summary\n\nThis task completes workspace tenancy beyond create/list/detail (MARATHON-5): authenticated **members** can list workspace membership; **admins/owners** can PATCH `name` / `join_mode`; **admins/owners** can change other members’ roles under a strict hierarchy; and the **owner** alone can transfer ownership to an existing **admin** (previous owner becomes admin). It locks `MemberObj`, the four REST seams SPA settings (MARATHON-18) and channel/invitation authz (MARATHON-7/12) depend on, and keeps `workspaces.owner_id` synchronized with `workspace_members.role='owner'`.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n### Decision\n\n| Concern | Choice |\n|--------|--------|\n| Persistence | Existing migration **v2** tables only — **no new migration** |\n| Route layout | Extend `server/workspaces/routes.py` under `/api/workspaces` |\n| Auth | Reuse `get_current_user`; all endpoints require Bearer |\n| Workspace resolution | Resolve by `slug`; unknown slug **or** non-member → **404** `not_found` (same as MARATHON-5; do not leak invite-only existence) |\n| Privilege failures | When caller **is** a member but lacks permission → **403** `forbidden` (first use of the code reserved by MARATHON-5) |\n| Ownership SoT | Dual write: `workspaces.owner_id` **and** exactly one `workspace_members.role='owner'`; transfer updates both in one transaction |\n| Role changes | `PATCH .../members/{user_id}` never creates owners; only `POST .../transfer_ownership` promotes to owner |\n| Member list enrichment | Join `users` for `username` / `display_name` (SPA settings without N+1 `GET /api/users/{id}`) |\n| Events / Redis | No pub/sub in this task (MARATHON-13/14); no Redis SoT |\n\n### Why\n\n- Schema already has `workspace_members.role` CHECK and `join_mode`; inventing a third ownership table would diverge from MARATHON-5.\n- Separating transfer from role PATCH prevents “set role to owner” shortcuts and matches the task’s 400/403 matrix.\n- Flat `MemberObj` with denormalized username/display_name matches MARATHON-5’s preference for flat workspace JSON while giving MARATHON-18 enough to render role rows.\n- 404-for-non-members preserves the privacy rule already shipped on workspace detail.\n\n### Alternatives considered\n\n| Alternative | Rejected because |\n|------------|------------------|\n| Nested full `UserObj` on every member | Heavier than needed; status/avatar can be fetched via MARATHON-4 when UI wants them |\n| Allow `role: \"owner\"` on PATCH | Task mandates **400**; ownership must go through transfer |\n| Soft-fail admin→admin PATCH as no-op 200 | Task mandates **403** |\n| New migration for `updated_at` on membership | Unused by contracts; keep v2 as-is |\n| Transfer to any member (auto-promote) | Task: target **must already be admin** else **400** |\n| Distinct 404 vs 403 for non-members on privileged routes | Would leak membership; keep 404 for non-members |\n\n### Trade-off accepted\n\nTests (and future join/invite flows) must **seed** non-owner members via SQL or later APIs until MARATHON-7/12 land. We accept that friction rather than inventing a provisional “add member” route here.\n\n### Conventions inherited (must not diverge)\n\nFrom **MARATHON-2**: `/api/...` JSON; shared SQLite WAL; Redis not SoT.\n\nFrom **MARATHON-3**: UUID4 string ids; UTC Z timestamps in DB; `ApiError` + `{error:{code,message}}`; ignore unknown JSON body fields; `400` `validation_error`; `401` `unauthorized`; `409` `conflict` only for uniqueness (not used by these endpoints unless CHECK fails unexpectedly).\n\nFrom **MARATHON-4**: `UserObj` fields `username` / `display_name` semantics; PATCH uses `exclude_unset` (omit = leave unchanged).\n\nFrom **MARATHON-5**: `WorkspaceObj` exact keys; `validate_name` / slug rules; membership gate → 404; `join_mode` ∈ {`open`,`invite_only`}; roles ∈ {`owner`,`admin`,`member`,`guest`}; `forbidden` reserved for role rules.\n\n## Interface Contracts\n\n### Shared types\n\n#### `WorkspaceObj` (uncha\n... [truncated]\n\nNotes:\nDesign complete for workspace members/roles/transfer. No new migration (reuse v2). Contracts: GET members → MemberObj{user_id,role,username,display_name}; PATCH /{slug} name/join_mode admin|owner; PATCH members/{id} role with matrix (admin cannot touch admin/owner; role=owner→400; owner target→403); POST transfer_ownership owner→admin only, previous owner→admin. Non-members→404; privilege→403. Handed to review; implementer should keep backend label.\n\n\n### Step 2: Ground Yourself Before Implementing\n\n#### 2a. Read the Epic\n- Determine the parent epic from the task ID or the parent field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'\n- Run 'loom data show <epic-id>' and read its notes — the conventions there are **authoritative**\n\n#### 2b. Read Dependencies and Siblings\n- Check depends_on in 'loom data show <id> --output json'. A dependency still open means Step 6a.\n- Read two or three closed siblings in the epic. Extract their error semantics, naming, validation placement, and transaction boundaries — and match them.\n\n#### 2c. Read the Design and Reconcile Against the Code\n- Read the whole design: contract, data model, edge cases, acceptance criteria\n- Open every file the design names and read it as it stands today; run 'git log --oneline -5 -- <file>' to see whether a sibling already moved it\n- Follow the design's **intent** against the code's **current** conventions. If the design's approach cannot work at all, go to Step 6b.\n\n#### 2d. Read the Neighborhood\nRead the handlers, services, and repositories next to the ones you are touching. Copy their layering, their error wrapping, and their logging conventions.\n\n### Step 3: Implement\n\n- Implement the contract exactly as designed — field names and types included\n- Validate input at the boundary, and return the error shapes the design specifies\n- Handle every failure path the design lists: a swallowed error is a defect even when the happy path passes\n- Keep changes minimal and inside scope. Do not refactor code you merely read.\n- Schema changes go in a migration file, forward and reversible, in its own commit before the code that uses it\n- **No TODO comments for deferred work.** Record follow-ups in task notes so they become tracked work.\n\n### Step 4: Test\n\n- Write or extend tests for every behavior you changed. Cover the failure paths and the edge cases the design lists, not only the happy path.\n- Each test must fail without your change. A test that passes either way tests nothing.\n- Run the project's full test command and fix what you broke.\n- Exercise the change at the real boundary too: call the endpoint or the entry point and check the actual response body and status. Do not assume it works because the unit test is green.\n- Before starting a command that binds the app's fixed ports, run `marathon-freeports` if a port is busy, then wrap the command as `marathon-portlock <cmd>`. Four workers share the ports and the lock serializes them.\n- Kill every server you started before signalling completion. Never leave a server running.\n- If the task included a migration: apply it against a local development database, verify the resulting schema, and verify the reverse direction. Never run a migration against a shared or production database.\n\n### Step 5: Review Your Own Change\n\n- Walk every acceptance criterion in the design, including the negative ones\n- Re-read the diff for scope creep, debug output, and swallowed errors\n- Confirm the contract you shipped matches the contract the design wrote, character for character on names and types\n\n### Step 6: If You Cannot Finish\n\n**Step 6a — External blocker** (an unfinished dependency, a third-party approval, a bug outside this scope):\n```\nloom data update <id> --status blocked --notes \"BLOCKED: <reason + any blocking task ID>\"\n```\nCommit meaningful partial work first, then run 'loom complete' and EXIT.\n\n**Step 6b — The design is unviable** (the work can proceed, but the design must change first):\n```\nloom data update <id> --notes \"NEEDS-REVISION: <what is wrong + the concrete direction for the next version + evidence>\"\nloom data update <id> --status open --add-label needs-revision --add-label architect --assignee \"\"\n```\nAdding both labels routes the task back to the architect. Commit salvageable work, run 'loom complete', and EXIT.\n\nIf you cannot tell which applies, prefer 6b.\n\n### Step 7: Deliver Through the Harness Gate\n\n- Re-run the project's build and test commands. Do NOT deliver with anything failing.\n- Commit in order, migrations first: `git add <migration files> && git commit -m \"migration: <what changes> (<task-id>)\"`, then `git add <code files> && git commit -m \"<brief description> (<task-id>)\"`\n- Stage specific paths only — never `git add -A` or `git add .`\n- Record anything the reviewer needs (a deviation, a discovered gap) in the task notes\n- Determine your attempt number: count existing comments containing `IMPL-DONE` on this task (from `loom data show <id> --output json`) and add 1\n- Record the completion signal (REQUIRED, exact shape):\n  `loom data comment <id> \"IMPL-DONE attempt=<n> commit=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\"`\n- Move the task to review and release it:\n  `loom data update <id> --status review --assignee \"\"`\n- NEVER close the task yourself — the harness gate integrates and closes\n- Signal completion: `loom complete`\n\n### CRITICAL: STOP\n\nAfter Step 6 or Step 7 you are DONE.\n- Do NOT pick up another task\n- Do NOT keep going\n- Simply EXIT\n\nYou completed ONE task. You will be run again for the next one."}]},"session_id":"6d466b8b-84ad-4a35-92ac-b8e56b93b2c8"}
